LESSONS_I7 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Stock health: scope and reads", "11", "<p>This module covers stock health and analytics in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: run the stock aging read and maintain the over-ninety-day working list; classify items by movement and combine the classification with value ranking; run sell-through analytics at network and site level; operate the dead-stock lanes in their preference order with their documents; apply the upstream prevention reads; compute stock turns and compile the monthly health page.</p><p><b>What stock health means.</b> Modules 1-6 keep the records accurate. This module reads those records to answer a different question: is the stock itself in good condition as an investment? Stock that does not move ties up money, occupies space, approaches expiry, and eventually writes off. The reads in this module identify that stock early, while the corrective lanes still have options; the lanes then move, reprice, return, or write off the identified stock by document.</p><p><b>The read-and-lane structure.</b> Each read produces a list; each list feeds a lane; each lane is a documented procedure with an owner and an approval level. The reads are scheduled, not occasional: a stock health problem identified at month three costs a markdown; the same problem identified at month eleven costs a write-off.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The stock manager runs the reads and prepares the lists; the owner decides the lanes at the monthly review for items above the threshold; the stock function executes the decided lanes through the standard documents (modules 2, 3, and 6). Purchasing receives the upstream findings (Chapter 6), because most stock health problems are purchasing decisions read back three months later.</p><p><b>The data precondition.</b> Every read in this module computes from the ledger and the movement history. The reads are reliable to the degree modules 1-5 are operated: accurate quantities (module 4's counts), honest dates (module 2's posting discipline), correct values (module 5). A site with weak record discipline reads its stock health from fiction; the accuracy metric (module 4) is therefore the first figure on the health page, because it states how much the other figures can be trusted.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the stock aging read. Chapter 3: movement classification. Chapter 4: sell-through analytics. Chapter 5: the dead-stock lanes. Chapter 6: upstream prevention. Chapter 7: turns, margin, and the health page. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The stock aging read", "12", "<p>The aging read groups stock value by how long it has been held. This chapter is the report, the bands, and the working list.</p><p><b>1. The Stock Ageing report.</b> Open Stock Ageing (awesome bar) — the standing report over the ledger's receipt history. Per item per warehouse it computes the age of the held stock from the receipt dates of the quantities still on hand (the earliest-in quantities age first, consistent with the receipt-queue arithmetic), and presents the age columns with quantities and values.</p><p><b>2. The band structure.</b> The policy sets the bands; the standing shape for a trading business: 0-30 days, 31-60, 61-90, and over 90. For a pharmaceutical distributor the bands read alongside module 3's expiry horizons — age and remaining shelf life are different measures (a slow item bought with long dating ages without expiry risk; a fast item bought short-dated expires without aging), and the two reads are run separately for that reason.</p><p><b>3. The monthly compile.</b> With the close, per warehouse and for the network:</p><p>1. <b>The value per band</b> — the aging profile: how much of the stock investment sits in each band.<br>2. <b>The over-ninety-day line as a percentage of total stock value</b> — the single-figure trend. The percentage is the comparable number across months and sites; the policy sets the target and the review threshold.<br>3. <b>The over-ninety-day working list</b> — every item in the band with quantity, value, warehouse, and last movement date. This list is the module's primary working document: Chapter 5's lanes work through it item by item at the monthly review.</p><p><b>4. Reading the profile.</b> Three standing observations the compile is checked for:</p><p>1. <b>A growing over-ninety band</b> — stock entering the band faster than the lanes clear it: either the lanes are not running or purchasing is over-buying (Chapter 6's feedback loop).<br>2. <b>Band concentration by item group</b> — one group carrying a disproportionate share of the aged value directs the review to a range or supplier decision rather than item-by-item treatment.<br>3. <b>Band concentration by site</b> — aged stock concentrated at one branch feeds module 6's rebalancing read before any markdown is considered: stock aged at one site may sell at its normal price at another.</p><p><b>5. The read's discipline.</b> The working list carries forward month to month with each item's lane decision and status. An item appearing on the list for a second month without a decision is an escalation to the owner per the review procedure — the list exists to force decisions, and an undecided item is the list failing at its purpose.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Movement classification", "12", "<p>Value ranking (module 4's ABC) and movement speed are different measurements. This chapter combines them into the two-dimensional read that sets priorities.</p><p><b>1. The movement classes.</b> Per item per site, computed from consumption over the policy's window (the standing window: two quarters):</p><p>1. <b>Fast</b> — consistent movement across the window at or above the group's median.<br>2. <b>Slow</b> — movement present but below the median, or irregular across the window.<br>3. <b>Dead</b> — negligible or zero movement across the window. The policy defines negligible numerically (for example, fewer than a set number of units across the window), so the classification is computed, not judged.</p><p><b>2. Why value ranking alone is insufficient.</b> Module 4's ABC classification ranks by consumption value and directs counting effort. A C-class item — low individual value — can be dead in large numbers: individually each line is small, and collectively the class ties up significant value and shelf space while attracting no attention precisely because each line is small. The movement classification exists to read what value ranking passes over.</p><p><b>3. The two-dimensional read.</b> The classes cross into a priority grid, worked in this order at the review:</p><p>1. <b>High-value dead</b> (A or B class, dead movement) — the priority cell: significant money not moving. Each line is worked individually through Chapter 5's lanes at the first review it appears.<br>2. <b>Low-value dead in volume</b> (C class, dead movement) — worked as a set: the count of lines and their aggregate value are the review figures, and the treatment is usually collective — a range decision, a clearance programme, or the delisting flow (module 8) — rather than line-by-line handling.<br>3. <b>High-value slow</b> — the watch cell: reorder levels checked against the movement (Chapter 6) before more arrives.<br>4. <b>Fast items</b> — no health action; their reads belong to replenishment (module 6).</p><p><b>4. The classification cadence.</b> Computed quarterly alongside the ABC review (module 4's cadence), stored per item per site, and compared against the prior quarter: items newly entering the dead class are the early-warning list — a line that was slow last quarter and dead this quarter is identified two quarters earlier than the aging read alone would surface it, because aging measures the stock already held while movement measures the demand that stopped.</p><p><b>5. The classification's limits, stated.</b> Seasonal items classify as dead in their off season by construction; the classification read carries a seasonal flag per the range list, and flagged items are assessed against the same window of the prior year (Chapter 4's like-window rule) rather than the preceding quarter. New items inside their probation window (Chapter 6) are excluded from the dead class until the probation read completes.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Sell-through analytics", "12", "<p>Module 6 defined the eight-week sell-through read for replenishment. This chapter extends it into the analytical views the health reviews use.</p><p><b>1. The base figure, restated.</b> Sell-through per item per site: units sold across the window as a percentage of units available across the window (opening stock plus receipts). The figure states demand against stocking, and it is comparable across items, sites, and periods — the property the views below depend on.</p><p><b>2. The network view.</b> The same item's sell-through across all sites, side by side:</p><p>1. <b>Uniform low</b> — the item is not selling anywhere: a range or pricing question for the review, and a candidate for Chapter 5's lanes.<br>2. <b>Divergent</b> — selling at one site, standing at another: module 6's rebalancing case, proposed as a lateral with the figures attached. The divergence read runs before any markdown decision, because moving stock to where it sells at full price precedes discounting it where it does not.<br>3. <b>Uniform high with stockouts</b> — the replenishment question (module 6's reorder health read), not a health problem.</p><p><b>3. The group rollup.</b> Sell-through aggregated by item group: the view that supports range decisions. A group whose sell-through declines across two consecutive windows while its stock value holds or grows is accumulating aged stock by arithmetic — the compile flags it before the aging read shows the result, and the finding routes to the range review (Chapter 6) with the purchasing data attached.</p><p><b>4. The like-window rule.</b> Comparisons run against comparable periods: this eight-week window against the same window of the prior year for seasonal ranges, against the preceding window for stable ranges. The range list marks which items are seasonal (Chapter 3's flag); the compile applies the correct comparison per the flag. A seasonal item compared against its own peak reads as collapsing; compared against last year's same season it reads correctly, and the rule exists because the wrong comparison generates false entries on the review lists.</p><p><b>5. The compile discipline.</b> The sell-through views compile monthly with the close, from the standard reports, by the documented procedure — the same figures every month, computed the same way, filed with the review pack. The reviews depend on trend, and trend depends on consistent computation: a figure computed differently between months produces movement that is method, not demand. Where the computation changes (a window resized, a group restructured), the change is recorded and the affected trend restarts from the change date.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The dead-stock lanes", "13", "<p>Identified dead stock is resolved through one of five lanes, in preference order, each by document. This chapter is the lanes and the monthly review that assigns them.</p><p><b>1. Lane 1 — rebalancing.</b> The item sells at another site (Chapter 4's divergence read): a lateral transfer per module 6's procedure — proposed with the sell-through figures, approved at the center, executed as a standard transfer with identity on the rows. Rebalancing recovers full price and costs a transfer; it is first in preference for that reason.</p><p><b>2. Lane 2 — markdown.</b> The item sells at a lower price: the markdown per the concession machinery (the ZhiftPOS track's markdown reasons and approval rules), with the dead-stock reason code, the price set per the policy's markdown ladder, and the review tracking sell-through at the new price. A markdown that does not move the stock within its review window escalates to the next lane rather than deepening indefinitely — the ladder's steps and windows are written in the policy.</p><p><b>3. Lane 3 — return to vendor.</b> Supplier terms permit returns on the line: the Procurement track's return flow, initiated within the terms' window, batches named on the return, credit tracked to settlement. This lane's availability is a purchasing term decided at supplier onboarding; the health review's recurring use of it on one supplier's lines is feedback for the next negotiation (Chapter 6).</p><p><b>4. Lane 4 — bundling and substitution.</b> The item moves as part of an offer or as a recommended substitute per the selling policy: the bundle or promotion machinery (the ZhiftPOS track's promotion configuration), with the dead-stock lines named in the offer's file. The lane applies where the item supports another sale even though it does not sell alone.</p><p><b>5. Lane 5 — write-off.</b> The remaining case: no site sells it, no price moves it, no return applies, no offer carries it. The write-off posts through module 2's issue lane — the reason, the account, the evidence (the review's decision note), the approval per the value ladder — and the disposal follows the compliance rules where the goods are regulated. The write-off is a decided expense: it appears on the review's decision list, the owner approves it per the band, and the month's issues read (module 2) reports it with the other decided figures.</p><p><b>6. The monthly dead-stock review.</b> The standing meeting, with the close: the over-ninety working list (Chapter 2) and the dead classification (Chapter 3) consolidated; each item or set assigned its lane with a one-line reason; prior months' assignments checked for status (the markdown that moved, the return awaiting credit, the lateral completed); undecided second-month items escalated per Chapter 2's rule. The review's output is the decision list — item, value, lane, owner, date — filed with the health page, and every decision on it is executable through a standard document the same week.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Upstream prevention", "12", "<p>The lanes resolve dead stock; this chapter reduces how much is created. Four upstream reads, each routing findings to the function that acts on them.</p><p><b>1. Reorder level hygiene.</b> Module 6's quarterly reorder health read, applied with the health data: levels on items whose movement class fell (Chapter 3's early-warning list) are corrected the same quarter — a level set for a fast item continues ordering fast-item quantities into a slowing demand until someone corrects it, and the correction is the cheapest prevention in the module. The read closes with the corrections written and dated.</p><p><b>2. Purchasing feedback.</b> Two standing figures route to the procurement owner monthly:</p><p>1. <b>The aging profile by supplier</b> — aged stock grouped by the supplier it was bought from: a supplier whose lines recur in the over-ninety band is a buying-quantity or line-selection question for the next purchasing review.<br>2. <b>The expiry horizon profile</b> (module 3's quarterly read) — the short-dated band's trend as purchasing feedback: buying outrunning sell-through shows here first for dated goods.</p><p>Both route as data with items and figures attached, per the standing data-not-editorial rule; the purchasing decisions belong to the Procurement track's reviews.</p><p><b>3. The range review and delisting.</b> Twice yearly, from the group rollups (Chapter 4) and the dead classification (Chapter 3): lines the business decides to stop stocking exit through the delisting flow — remaining stock resolved through the lanes, reorder rows removed, the item retired per module 8's item lifecycle procedure (disabled at zero, never deleted). A delisting decided but not executed leaves reorder machinery ordering a discontinued line; the review's decision list therefore carries execution status until each line closes.</p><p><b>4. The new-item probation read.</b> Every newly ranged item carries a probation window (the policy's figure — the standing shape: one quarter): stocked at the opening estimate, read weekly against sell-through in its first window per module 6's new-branch discipline applied at item scale, and confirmed, adjusted, or exited at the probation review. The read exists because new items are estimates by definition, and an estimate reviewed at week twelve costs at most one quarter's mis-stocking; an estimate never reviewed becomes the aging read's finding a year later at the full cost.</p><p><b>5. The prevention summary on the health page.</b> One line per read on the monthly page (Chapter 7): reorder corrections made, the supplier aging figure, delisting execution status, probation outcomes. The summary states whether prevention is operating; a health page that reports only the lanes is reporting the cost of prevention not running.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Turns, margin, and the health page", "12", "<p>The module's output figures, and the one page that carries them monthly.</p><p><b>1. Stock turns.</b> The standing efficiency figure: cost of goods sold across the period divided by average stock value across the period, annualised per the policy's convention. Computed per item group and for the whole:</p><p>1. <b>The group comparison</b> — groups compared against their own history and against the policy's expectation per group (fast-moving retail groups turn faster than equipment ranges by nature; the expectation is set per group, not globally).<br>2. <b>The trend</b> — turns improving means stock working harder: the same sales carried on less investment. The figure summarises the whole module's effect in one number, which is why it leads the health page.<br>3. <b>The computation source</b> — cost of goods sold from the accounts (module 5's perpetual postings), average stock from the Stock Balance history; both figures from the standard reports by the documented method, per Chapter 4's compile discipline.</p><p><b>2. The margin read, joined.</b> Module 5's monthly margin-by-group read joins the health view here: a group with declining turns and stable margin is over-stocked; a group with stable turns and declining margin has a cost or pricing movement (module 5's territory); a group declining on both is a range question for Chapter 6's review. The joined reading directs each finding to its own procedure — the health page presents the figures side by side for exactly this reading.</p><p><b>3. The monthly health page.</b> One page, with the close, to the owner:</p><p>1. <b>Record reliability</b> — the count accuracy metric per site (module 4): the figure that states how much the rest of the page can be trusted.<br>2. <b>Total stock value and turns</b> — the investment and its efficiency, trended.<br>3. <b>The aging profile</b> — value per band and the over-ninety percentage, trended (Chapter 2).<br>4. <b>The dead-stock position</b> — the working list's total value, the month's lane decisions and completions, the write-off total against its approvals (Chapter 5).<br>5. <b>The prevention summary</b> — Chapter 6's one-liners.</p><p><b>4. The page's discipline.</b> The same five sections every month, computed the same way, one page in length. The page is the owner's standing answer to the condition of the stock investment; its trends are the module's evidence that the reads and lanes are working; and its preparation is a documented procedure the stock manager's deputy can execute, per the programme's handover standard.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: HealthTrade's stock health year", "13", "<p>This chapter runs Chapters 2-7 through HealthTrade's first stock health year. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the first aging compile (Chapter 2).</b> The first monthly compile establishes the baseline: the over-ninety-day band holds <b>₦6.2m — 9.6% of total stock value</b> — concentrated at Central, with the working list running to several pages. The figure and the list go to the first monthly review with the band profile.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the two-dimensional read (Chapter 3).</b> The movement classification computes alongside: the priority cell (high-value dead) holds a short list worked individually; the volume cell holds <b>140 C-class SKUs with negligible movement across two quarters</b> — individually small lines, collectively a material value and a shelf-space cost, invisible to the value-ranked reads. The set is worked collectively per the procedure: a clearance programme for the bulk, and the delisting flow proposed for the tail.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the lanes run (Chapters 4-5).</b></p><p>1. <b>Rebalancing first:</b> the divergence read finds aged Central stock selling normally at the branches — <b>₦1.1m of the aged Akure-range allocation moves by two laterals</b> to the sites where the sell-through data shows demand, recovering full price for a transfer's cost.<br>2. <b>Markdown second:</b> the named clearance lines enter the markdown ladder with the dead-stock reason code; the review window tracks movement at each step.<br>3. <b>Vendor returns:</b> two suppliers' lines return within terms, batches named, credits tracked to settlement; one supplier's recurring appearance files as negotiation feedback.<br>4. <b>The residual write-off:</b> the lines no lane moved post through the issue lane at the year's third review — a decided figure with the review's note attached, approved per the band.</p><p><b>Step 4 — prevention engages (Chapter 6).</b> Reorder corrections write for every early-warning line the quarter it appears; the supplier aging figure starts routing monthly; the range review executes the first delisting batch through the retirement flow with execution status tracked to zero; the probation read exits two new lines at week twelve at one quarter's cost each.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the year's trend (Chapter 7).</b> The health page runs twelve months on the same five sections. Year end against baseline: the over-ninety percentage below its opening figure and trending down; turns per group improved on the retail ranges as the cleared value converts to working stock; the write-off total for the year on the page against its approvals — a decided expense with its documents, arrived at after the cheaper lanes ran first; and the accuracy metric above target at every site, stating the page's own reliability. The year's file — twelve pages, twelve decision lists, the lane documents — answers any question about any line's treatment in one lookup.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in stock health, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — reads without lanes.</b> Symptom: aging reports compiled and filed; the over-ninety band growing month over month; no decisions attached. Correction: the monthly review instituted, the working list worked item by item. Prevention: the read-and-lane structure — every list feeds a lane, and second-month undecided items escalate.</p><p><b>Error 2 — markdown as the first response.</b> Symptom: aged stock discounted at the site it aged at while other sites sell it at full price. Correction: the divergence read run first; laterals where the data supports them. Prevention: the lane preference order — rebalancing before markdown, in the written procedure.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the invisible C-class accumulation.</b> Symptom: value-ranked reads showing no problem while hundreds of small dead lines accumulate. Correction: the two-dimensional read computed; the volume cell worked collectively. Prevention: the quarterly movement classification alongside the ABC review.</p><p><b>Error 4 — seasonal false positives.</b> Symptom: off-season items entering the dead lists; lanes run against demand that returns; the review lists losing credibility. Correction: the seasonal flags applied; comparisons re-run like for like. Prevention: the like-window rule and the range list's seasonal marking.</p><p><b>Error 5 — lanes without upstream fixes.</b> Symptom: the same items cleared and re-accumulating; purchasing unaware of the aging its orders create. Correction: the prevention reads instituted and routed. Prevention: reorder hygiene the same quarter, the supplier aging figure monthly, and the probation read on every new line.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the inconsistent compile.</b> Symptom: health figures computed differently between months; trends showing method changes as demand changes; reviews arguing about the numbers instead of the stock. Correction: the computation documented; affected trends restarted from the change date. Prevention: the compile discipline — the same figures, the same method, filed with the pack.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: run the aging read with its bands, the over-ninety percentage, and the carried working list; compute the movement classes and the two-dimensional grid with the seasonal and probation exclusions; run the sell-through views — network, divergence, group rollup — under the like-window rule; operate the five lanes in preference order with their documents and the monthly review's decision list; apply the four prevention reads with their routings; and compute turns, join the margin read, and compile the five-section health page.</p><p><b>Module rule 7: stock health is read on schedule and resolved by lane — aging and movement compiled monthly by the documented method, every listed item assigned a lane with its document, rebalancing before markdown and write-off last, findings routed upstream to purchasing and the range review, and the whole reported on one page whose first figure is the accuracy of the records it stands on.</b> Module 8 covers settings, roles, and structure.</p>"),
]

